# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the server and conformance suites under the
# `thread sanitizer` job (.github/workflows/server.yml; TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=...).
#
# Every entry hides reports, so each names known-benign behaviour in third-party code
# (DuckDB internals, OpenSSL, the C++ runtime) with the reason. Never a Hardcharger or
# warp-sql symbol: a race there is a bug to fix, not to suppress. Syntax:
#   race:<symbol or file substring>   thread_leak:<...>   deadlock:<...>
# https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions

# jemalloc, DuckDB's allocator (DuckDB builds it in on Linux): its thread caches and
# extent metadata (tcache.c tcache_bin_flush_impl_small, edata.h edata_nfree_inc/sub,
# base.c base_alloc_edata) are managed with its own lock-free protocol, which TSan cannot
# model, so every allocation-heavy run reports races whose top frames are all jemalloc.
# The standard jemalloc suppression (GizmoSQL's tests/tsan.supp has the same).
race:third_party/jemalloc

# DuckDB storage: a commit appends rows into a transient column segment
# (ColumnSegment::Append -> ValidityAppend/SetInvalidUnsafe) while another thread scans
# the same segment (ValidityScan/AlignedScan). DuckDB publishes the committed row count
# under the row group lock and readers never look past it, so the overlapping bytes are
# never used; TSan cannot see that invariant. Seen with the instrumentation writer
# appending while a reader scans its table.
race:duckdb::ColumnSegment::Append

# DuckDB hash joins: right semi/anti joins mark build-side rows as matched from every
# probing thread at once (ScanStructure::NextRightSemiOrAntiJoin and the
# MarkChainsAsFoundLoop it calls: Load<bool>/Store<bool> on the row's match flag). The only
# value ever written is `true`, and the flags are read after the probe phase finishes; the
# DuckDB source says so beside the store ("threadsan reports this as a data race ...").
# Seen in the TPC-H suites. GizmoSQL's tests/tsan.supp suppresses all of ScanStructure.
race:duckdb::JoinHashTable::ScanStructure::NextRightSemiOrAntiJoin

# libpq (PostgreSQL 18.6, built by server/scripts/build-extension-deps.sh, used by the
# postgres scanner and DuckLake's PostgreSQL metadata): every connection's startup saves
# client_encoding and standard_conforming_strings into the process-wide statics
# static_client_encoding / static_std_strings (src/interfaces/libpq/fe-exec.c:1151 and
# :1156, in pqSaveParameterStatus), so two threads connecting at once race on them. Only
# the deprecated connection-less PQescapeString and PQescapeBytea read them (fe-exec.c:4229
# and :4605; the source's comment: "at least in single-connection-using programs"); no
# code in the server, warp-sql, DuckDB or its built-in extensions calls either, and every
# connection keeps its own copy in its PGconn. Seen in TSan CI with two sessions' statements
# opening pool connections to one DuckLake-on-PostgreSQL catalog at the same moment.
race:pqSaveParameterStatus

# The TSan-only fixture (scripts/build-tsan-duckdb.sh) instruments and links httpfs,
# ducklake, postgres_scanner, tpch and autocomplete into libduckdb. Production builds
# and ASan still test their signed runtime modules, including repository replacement.
# Never suppress the races those modules would create by mixing uninstrumented atomic
# synchronization with the instrumented engine.
#
# Not suppressed: extensions downloaded from DuckDB's repository (INSTALL spatial) are
# prebuilt without TSan and carry their own copy of DuckDB's code. Loading one while
# another thread plans a query reports a race between ExtensionCallbackManager::Register
# / ~ExtensionCallbackRegistry (in the extension's binary) and Optimizer::Optimize's read
# of the registry it replaced (src/main/extension_callback_manager.cpp: copy-on-write
# under atomic_load/atomic_store): the reference count that orders the free after the read
# is decremented in uninstrumented code. Not a DuckDB bug; tests that need such an
# extension load it in the init SQL, before the server runs anything else
# (test_geoarrow.py), so nothing races it.
